WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.813 --> 00:00:11.714
Mr. Phileas Fogg was a mystery.

2
00:00:12.575 --> 00:00:16.397
No one knew how he had made his fortune, but there was no doubt that he was rich.

3
00:00:17.857 --> 00:00:21.559
He was in his forties, tall, well-built, with handsome features.

4
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.722
He had light hair, pale skin, and excellent teeth.

5
00:00:26.782 --> 00:00:31.790
He was one of the most prominent members of London's Reform Club, and yet he attracted little attention.

6
00:00:32.931 --> 00:00:36.034
It was clear to those who knew him that Phileas Fogg was a traveler.

7
00:00:37.455 --> 00:00:41.539
He was familiar with the world and knew the details of even the most remote areas of the

8
00:00:41.599 --> 00:00:45.262
earth. It was also certain, however, that Mr.

9
00:00:45.303 --> 00:00:47.424
Fogg had not left London in many years.

10
00:00:49.006 --> 00:00:53.831
He lived a quiet single life with no wife or children to keep him company, and his daily

11
00:00:53.871 --> 00:00:55.132
routine never changed.

12
00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.242
On October 2, 1872, Mr.

13
00:00:59.302 --> 00:01:02.944
Fogg sat squarely in his armchair in his mansion on Savile Row.

14
00:01:04.345 --> 00:01:07.086
His hands were resting on his knees, and his body was straight.

15
00:01:08.547 --> 00:01:15.271
He was watching a complicated clock that told the seconds, minutes, hours, days, months, and years all at

16
00:01:15.411 --> 00:01:20.294
once. A knock sounded at the front door, and before long, Mr.

17
00:01:20.334 --> 00:01:23.036
Fogg's servant, who was leaving that day, appeared.

18
00:01:24.224 --> 00:01:27.266
"The new servant is here to see you, sir," he said.

19
00:01:29.108 --> 00:01:32.450
Then a young man about thirty years old entered the room and bowed.

20
00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:37.534
"You are a Frenchman, I believe," asked Phileas Fogg, "and your name is John?"

21
00:01:37.554 --> 00:01:41.918
"Jean, if monsieur pleases," replied the newcomer.

22
00:01:41.938 --> 00:01:44.620
"My name is Jean Passepartout.

23
00:01:46.221 --> 00:01:50.204
My surname suits me because I have a natural ability to move from one business to another.

24
00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.661
I believe I'm honest, monsieur, and I've had several jobs.

25
00:01:53.761 --> 00:01:59.683
I've been a singer, a circus acrobat, a tight-rope walker, a gymnastics teacher, and finally, a fireman in

26
00:01:59.743 --> 00:02:02.203
Paris." "Well, Mr.

27
00:02:02.244 --> 00:02:04.064
Passepartout," said Mr.

28
00:02:04.104 --> 00:02:06.185
Fogg, "you have been well recommended to me.

29
00:02:07.285 --> 00:02:08.686
I have heard good reports about you."

30
00:02:09.746 --> 00:02:11.747
"Thank you, monsieur," said Passepartout.

31
00:02:12.527 --> 00:02:16.148
"I wish now to enjoy a quiet domestic life as a valet here in England.

32
00:02:17.428 --> 00:02:20.829
I have heard that you are a most exact and settled gentleman, so I have come in the

33
00:02:20.889 --> 00:02:22.489
hope of living a tranquil life.

34
00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.070
Maybe I will even forget the name of Passepartout."

35
00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.431
"You know my conditions?"

36
00:02:28.851 --> 00:02:29.391
asked Mr.

37
00:02:29.431 --> 00:02:32.252
Fogg. "Yes, monsieur," said Passepartout.

38
00:02:32.272 --> 00:02:34.133
"Good! What time is it?"

39
00:02:34.513 --> 00:02:35.053
asked Mr.

40
00:02:35.093 --> 00:02:42.255
Fogg. Taking a huge silver watch from his pocket, Passepartout replied, "Twenty-five minutes after eleven."

41
00:02:42.275 --> 00:02:45.636
"Your watch is slow," said Mr.

42
00:02:45.676 --> 00:02:50.301
Fogg. "Pardon me, monsieur, it is impossible," said Passepartout.

43
00:02:50.321 --> 00:02:53.623
"It is four minutes too slow," said Mr.

44
00:02:53.663 --> 00:02:55.563
Fogg. "No matter.

45
00:02:56.284 --> 00:02:57.884
It's enough only to mention the error.

46
00:02:58.785 --> 00:03:05.688
Now from this moment, 29 minutes after 11 a.m., this Wednesday, the second of October, you are in

47
00:03:05.768 --> 00:03:06.448
my service."

48
00:03:08.329 --> 00:03:12.190
Mr. Fogg stood up, and without a further word, he put on his hat and left.

49
00:03:13.751 --> 00:03:16.672
Every day, at exactly 11:30 a.m., Mr.

50
00:03:16.712 --> 00:03:19.333
Fogg left his house and walked to the Reform Club.

51
00:03:20.493 --> 00:03:24.234
He was so precise that he never appeared to be rushed or agitated in any way.

52
00:03:25.475 --> 00:03:30.376
He was the most deliberate person in the world and always reached his destination at the very moment

53
00:03:30.416 --> 00:03:31.116
that he was due.

54
00:03:32.657 --> 00:03:37.358
At half-past eleven, then, Passepartout found himself alone in the house on Savile Row.

55
00:03:38.699 --> 00:03:42.740
He began an inspection and was pleased to find the house clean and arranged neatly.

56
00:03:43.724 --> 00:03:48.487
When Passepartout reached the second floor, he recognized at once the room that was meant for him.

57
00:03:50.828 --> 00:03:55.691
Hanging over the clock above the fireplace was the daily routine of the house, written on a card.

58
00:03:56.471 --> 00:03:59.893
It explained Passepartout's duties, from eight in the morning, when Mr.

59
00:03:59.933 --> 00:04:02.555
Fogg got up, until half-past eleven, when Mr.

60
00:04:02.595 --> 00:04:03.735
Fogg left for his club.

61
00:04:04.355 --> 00:04:10.719
It explained the tea and toast at 8:23, the shaving water at 9:37, and the washcloth, soap, and

62
00:04:10.759 --> 00:04:11.559
washbowl at 9:40.

63
00:04:13.696 --> 00:04:14.036
In Mr.

64
00:04:14.056 --> 00:04:17.419
Fogg's closet each pair of pants, coat, and vest had a number on it.

65
00:04:18.819 --> 00:04:21.721
This indicated the time of year and the season when it would be worn.

66
00:04:23.002 --> 00:04:25.864
Mr. Fogg used the same system for his shoes as well.

67
00:04:27.165 --> 00:04:31.187
Having inspected the house from top to bottom, Passepartout rubbed his hands together.

68
00:04:31.207 --> 00:04:34.549
"This is just what I wanted!"

69
00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:35.330
he said.

70
00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:37.451
"We will get along well together, Mr.

71
00:04:37.491 --> 00:04:38.032
Fogg and I!"

72
00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:40.540
Meanwhile Mr.

73
00:04:40.621 --> 00:04:46.963
Phileas Fogg had put his right foot before his left foot 575 times and his left foot before

74
00:04:47.003 --> 00:04:51.265
his right foot 576 times and reached the Reform Club.

75
00:04:52.626 --> 00:04:56.948
He immediately went into the dining room and took his place at his usual table, which had already

76
00:04:56.968 --> 00:04:57.688
been set for him.

77
00:04:59.609 --> 00:05:05.132
Phileas Fogg's lunch consisted of a broiled fish with sauce and a slice of roast beef garnished with

78
00:05:05.172 --> 00:05:10.188
mushrooms. He also ate a rhubarb and gooseberry tart and a morsel of Cheshire cheese.

79
00:05:11.929 --> 00:05:14.532
The entire meal was washed down with several cups of tea.

80
00:05:15.953 --> 00:05:19.536
Mr. Fogg rose at thirteen minutes to one and went into the large hall.

81
00:05:20.777 --> 00:05:26.002
He found his regular chair in his regular corner and happily sat down to pass the afternoon as

82
00:05:26.042 --> 00:05:32.788
he did every day: quietly reading the newspapers amidst the comfortable and lavish surroundings of his club.

83
00:05:36.844 --> 00:05:42.549
Hello. I am Jean Passepartout, and the year is 1872, during the Victorian Era.

84
00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:45.692
London is the largest and most modern city on earth.

85
00:05:47.253 --> 00:05:50.016
Its streets are very crowded with people and horse-drawn carriages.

86
00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:56.601
Below the streets, trains, which were invented less than fifty years ago, travel through the London Underground.

87
00:05:58.103 --> 00:06:00.285
A lot of great scientific discovery has occurred lately.

88
00:06:00.805 --> 00:06:03.948
At night London's streets are now lit by gas lamps.

89
00:06:05.384 --> 00:06:10.205
If you have an urgent message to send, you can send it by electronic wire, using Morse code.

90
00:06:10.725 --> 00:06:11.866
Perhaps you've heard of Morse code?

91
00:06:12.606 --> 00:06:16.087
It uses a series of dots and dashes to relay information.

92
00:06:18.047 --> 00:06:22.168
London is the capital not only of England but also of the vast British Empire.

93
00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:26.590
Queen Victoria rules over almost one-quarter of the earth's land.

94
00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:30.751
People often say, "The sun never sets on the British Empire."

95
00:06:31.771 --> 00:06:35.072
That's because it's always daytime in at least one of the Queen's territories.
